{
  "image_name": "0b64a6b784c8c83367daae1ce55eba5f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two ways a naïve T cell can generate different daughter-cell fates, centered on the regulator Ezh2 and its repression of memory genes. In the left concept, asymmetry arises within a single division. A naïve T cell contains regulatory factors, and during the first division these factors are distributed unequally between the two daughters. One daughter ends up with lower Ezh2, shown as weaker inhibition of memory genes, which favors a memory-cell fate. The other daughter has higher Ezh2, stronger repression of memory genes, and is biased toward an effector fate.\n\nOn the right, asymmetry is presented at the population level rather than within one mitosis. Here, external or internal signals first create cells with reduced Ezh2. Depending on which signal is received, subsequent division produces daughters that are collectively biased in one direction: one pathway yields progeny with low Ezh2 and active memory genes, favoring memory fate, whereas another yields progeny with high Ezh2 and suppressed memory genes, favoring effector fate.\n\nOverall, the diagram highlights Ezh2 as a switch-like regulator linking division history or signaling environment to divergent T-cell differentiation outcomes.",
